MAYOR OF PANGKALPINANG

MAYOR OF PANGKALPINANG

BANGKA BELITUNG ISLANDS PROVINCE

REGIONAL REGULATION OF PANGKALPINANG CITY

NUMBER 8 OF 2020

CONCERNING

EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF PANGKALPINANG,

Considering : a. that women as a national asset who play a role in the process of continuation and creation of quality generations need to receive guarantees for the fulfillment of their rights and Protection from acts of violence and discrimination;

b. that in order to provide Protection and means for self-actualization of women in society in Pangkalpinang City, it is necessary to have a regional regulation governing the empowerment and protection of women;

c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on Empowerment and Women;

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 28 of 1959 concerning Stipulation of Emergency Law Number 4 of 1959 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 55, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1091), Emergency Law Number 5 of 1956 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia

of 1956 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1091) and Emergency Law Number 57 (Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1091) concerning Establishment of Level II Regions including Municipal City Within Level I Region of South Sumatra as a Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1821);

3. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1);

4. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on The Elimination Of All Forms Of Discrimination Against Women) State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia

Number 3277);

5. Law Number 13 of 1998 concerning Elderly Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1998 Number 190, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3796);

6. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention No. 138 Concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment (ILO Convention Concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number

7. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);

8. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);

9. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence

(State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);

10. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);

11. Law Number 19 of 2011 concerning Ratification of the Convention On The Rights Of Persons With Disabilities (Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities), (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of

2011 Number 107, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5251);

12. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government to become a Law

(State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

13. Law Number 8 of 2016 concerning Persons with Disabilities (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 69, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5871);

14. Regional Regulation of Pangkalpinang City Number 18 of 2016 concerning Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus of Pangkalpinang City (Regional Gazette of Pangkalpinang City of 2016 Number 18);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PANGKALPINANG CITY and

MAYOR OF PANGKALPINANG

Article 2

(1) The Principles of Women's Empowerment are:

a. respect for human rights;
b. gender equality and justice;
c. protection of victims' rights; and
d. non-discriminatory.
(2) The objectives of Women's Empowerment and Protection are to:
a. ensure the fulfillment of women's rights so that they can live and participate optimally in accordance with human dignity;
b. provide gender justice and equality; c. provide protection and security for women victims of violence and discrimination, as well as crimes of trafficking in persons;
d. eliminate all forms of violence and discrimination against women;
e. provide services to women Victims of violence, and witnesses; and
f. provide recovery for women Victims of violence to increase the participation of women both individually and in groups as potential and resources in the conduct of social life.
(3) The scope of this Regional Regulation includes: a. women's rights;
b. obligations and responsibilities of regional government; c. women's empowerment;
d. women's protection;
e. protection of women victims of violence; f. prohibitions and sanctions;
g. community participation;
h. monitoring and evaluation; and i . funding.

Article 5

Every woman victim of violence has: a. the right to be respected for dignity as a human being;

b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the Victim;

c. the right to determine her own decision;

d. the right to obtain information;

e. the right to confidentiality;

f. the right to compensation;

g. the right to social rehabilitation;

h. the right to complaint handling; i the right of the Victim and her family to obtain facilities in the judicial process; and j . the right to assistance.

Article 36

(1) The community may participate in the implementation of women's empowerment and protection.

(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, groups or social community organizations.

(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of:

a. cooperation;
b. active role in policy formulation; and c. complaints/reports.
(4) The obligations and responsibilities of the community in the protection of women are implemented through activities:
a. conducting prevention of criminal acts;
b. providing protection to victims;
c. providing emergency assistance;
d. assisting in the process of submitting an application for protection determination; and
e. providing information or reporting the occurrence of violent criminal acts to law enforcement officers.
(5) Further provisions regarding community participation as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated in a Mayor Regulation.

Article 33

To provide Protection as referred to in Article 27, the City Regional Government may undertake efforts to:

a. provide services and handling as quickly as possible to witnesses and/or victims;
b. provide convenience, comfort, safety and free of charge for witnesses and/or victims;
c. maintain the confidentiality of witnesses and/or victims; d. guarantee justice and legal certainty for witnesses and/or victims;
e. provision of special service rooms at Police Offices;
f. provision of officers, health workers, social workers, and spiritual counselors;
g. creation and development of a system and mechanism for cooperation in service programs involving parties that are easily accessible to victims;
h. provide protection for companions, witnesses, family and friends of victims;
i. provide protection for female workers/laborers in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 23

(1) The implementation of protection for homeless women in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.

(2) Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) obtain the right to protection for:

a. adequate housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunity to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 18

The implementation of women's protection is given to: a. women workers/laborers;

b. elderly women;

c. women with disabilities;

d. homeless women;

e. women domestic workers;

f. women as family economic supporters; and g. women inmates of correctional institutions.

Article 17

The Mayor in implementing policies, programs and activities for women's protection makes efforts: a. coordination of the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for women's protection among regional apparatus; b. cooperation with regencies/cities within one province and cooperation between regencies/cities in other provinces in the implementation of policy programs, and activities for women's protection in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;

c. strengthening the institutional capacity of gender mainstreaming for the implementation of policy programs and activities for women's protection;

d. facilitation of women's protection services; e. provision of women's protection services; f. implementation of affirmative action for women's protection; and g. preparation of a data system for women's protection.

