Article 2

(1) The Principles of Women's Empowerment are:

a. respect for human rights;
b. gender equality and justice;
c. protection of victims' rights; and
d. non-discriminatory.
(2) The objectives of Women's Empowerment and Protection are to:
a. ensure the fulfillment of women's rights so that they can live and participate optimally in accordance with human dignity;
b. provide gender justice and equality; c. provide protection and security for women victims of violence and discrimination, as well as crimes of trafficking in persons;
d. eliminate all forms of violence and discrimination against women;
e. provide services to women Victims of violence, and witnesses; and
f. provide recovery for women Victims of violence to increase the participation of women both individually and in groups as potential and resources in the conduct of social life.
(3) The scope of this Regional Regulation includes: a. women's rights;
b. obligations and responsibilities of regional government; c. women's empowerment;
d. women's protection;
e. protection of women victims of violence; f. prohibitions and sanctions;
g. community participation;
h. monitoring and evaluation; and i . funding.

Article 3

(1) Every woman has the right to:

a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue descendants;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, virtuous, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process; f. be free from slavery or enslavement and threats;
g. obtain protection of personal self, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. obtain welfare and a decent life;
i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts;
k. freely choose a partner in marriage; and l. receive fair work wages in accordance with labor regulations.
(2) The right to participate in politics as referred to in paragraph (1) letter i is, women have the right to:
a. vote and/or be elected in general elections, regional head elections, or elections for political positions and other positions based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret voting; and
b. be appointed to any government position. (3) Every woman who is in a condition of disability, elderly, and/or pregnant, has the right to obtain facilities and special treatment.
(4) Every elderly woman, person with physical disability and/or mental disability, has the right to obtain care, non-formal education.

Article 33

To provide Protection as referred to in Article 27, the City Regional Government may undertake efforts to:

a. provide services and handling as quickly as possible to witnesses and/or victims;
b. provide convenience, comfort, safety and free of charge for witnesses and/or victims;
c. maintain the confidentiality of witnesses and/or victims; d. guarantee justice and legal certainty for witnesses and/or victims;
e. provision of special service rooms at Police Offices;
f. provision of officers, health workers, social workers, and spiritual counselors;
g. creation and development of a system and mechanism for cooperation in service programs involving parties that are easily accessible to victims;
h. provide protection for companions, witnesses, family and friends of victims;
i. provide protection for female workers/laborers in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 23

(1) The implementation of protection for homeless women in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.

(2) Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) obtain the right to protection for:

a. adequate housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunity to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 36

(1) The community may participate in the implementation of women's empowerment and protection.

(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, groups or social community organizations.

(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of:

a. cooperation;
b. active role in policy formulation; and c. complaints/reports.
(4) The obligations and responsibilities of the community in the protection of women are implemented through activities:
a. conducting prevention of criminal acts;
b. providing protection to victims;
c. providing emergency assistance;
d. assisting in the process of submitting an application for protection determination; and
e. providing information or reporting the occurrence of violent criminal acts to law enforcement officers.
(5) Further provisions regarding community participation as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated in a Mayor Regulation.

Article 5

Every woman victim of violence has: a. the right to be respected for dignity as a human being;

b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the Victim;

c. the right to determine her own decision;

d. the right to obtain information;

e. the right to confidentiality;

f. the right to compensation;

g. the right to social rehabilitation;

h. the right to complaint handling; i the right of the Victim and her family to obtain facilities in the judicial process; and j . the right to assistance.

Article 18

The implementation of women's protection is given to: a. women workers/laborers;

b. elderly women;

c. women with disabilities;

d. homeless women;

e. women domestic workers;

f. women as family economic supporters; and g. women inmates of correctional institutions.

